---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Hero from '~/components/widgets/Hero.astro';
import Content from '~/components/widgets/Content.astro';
import Features3 from '~/components/widgets/Features3.astro';
import CallToAction from '~/components/widgets/CallToAction.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Work Smarter with Gemini — Rise 360 Portfolio Course',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <Hero tagline="Instructional Design · Rise 360 · AI Adoption · Healthcare">
    <Fragment slot="title">
      Work Smarter with Gemini —<br />
      <span class="text-accent dark:text-white">Rise 360 Portfolio Course</span>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle">
      A self-paced Rise 360 course built as a portfolio piece — a practical, focused guide to creating Gemini Gems for
      employees at a large health organization, developed after completing Gemini Gem creation training at LearningMate.
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <!-- Context -->
  <Content tagline="The Project" title="From internal training to portfolio artifact" isReversed>
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full py-12">
        <div class="text-center">
          <p class="text-7xl font-bold font-heading text-accent leading-none">Gemini</p>
          <p class="text-xl text-muted mt-3">Rise 360 · Portfolio</p>
          <div class="mt-8 space-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
            <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Instructional Design · LearningMate</p>
            <p>Healthcare Context · Gemini Gems</p>
            <p>3 Sections · 9 Lessons</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">
        During Phase 1 of my SME contract with LearningMate — a content development engagement for the Chegg Skills web
        development certification program — LearningMate delivered internal Gemini training to all SMEs on the project.
        The training was a structured walkthrough of building custom AI assistants within Google Gemini for recurring
        tasks.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-4">
        Going through that training as a learner gave me a clear view of how the content could be adapted into a real
        workforce learning experience. I used it as the starting point for this portfolio piece: a stripped-down,
        practical Rise 360 course designed for employees at a large health organization — focused specifically on what
        someone in a healthcare workplace setting needs to know to build and use a Gem for a task they do every week.
      </p>
      <p>
        The healthcare framing shapes the content throughout — the examples, the use cases, and the tone all reflect the
        context of a large, compliance-aware organization where AI adoption needs to be practical, grounded, and
        trustworthy. The course keeps scope narrow by design: one tool, one use case, one real deliverable.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-b"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Course Sections -->
  <Features3
    tagline="Course Structure"
    title="Three sections, nine lessons"
    subtitle="The course moves from Gemini fundamentals to daily practice to building a custom Gem — keeping the scope narrow and the application direct."
    columns={3}
    items={[
      {
        title: 'What is Gemini and Why It Matters',
        description:
          "Introduces Gemini in a healthcare workplace context — what it is, what it's designed to help with, and why it's relevant to how staff already work. Includes an interactive element and knowledge check.",
        icon: 'tabler:bulb',
      },
      {
        title: 'Your First Prompts',
        description:
          'Covers the fundamentals of prompt writing — how to structure a prompt, what makes a prompt effective, and how to iterate. Grounded in scenarios that reflect real health organization workflows.',
        icon: 'tabler:message',
      },
      {
        title: 'Gemini in Your Daily Workflow',
        description:
          'Moves from isolated prompts to integrated practice — showing how Gemini fits into recurring tasks like drafting communications, summarizing documents, and preparing for meetings.',
        icon: 'tabler:briefcase',
      },
      {
        title: 'Best Practices and Getting Started',
        description:
          'Closes the first section with practical guidance — including accuracy habits, privacy and compliance considerations relevant to healthcare, and how to build confidence through low-stakes practice.',
        icon: 'tabler:checklist',
      },
      {
        title: 'What is a Gemini Gem?',
        description:
          'Introduces Gemini Gems — custom AI assistants built for specific recurring tasks. Covers what Gems are, when to use them, and what makes a strong Gem use case in a health organization context.',
        icon: 'tabler:robot',
      },
      {
        title: 'Setting Up Your Weekly Status Report Gem',
        description:
          'A step-by-step walkthrough of building a real Gem — a weekly status report assistant. Covers writing the system prompt, setting instructions, and configuring the Gem for consistent output.',
        icon: 'tabler:settings',
      },
      {
        title: 'Testing and Refining Your Gem',
        description:
          'Covers the iteration process — how to test a Gem with real inputs, evaluate the output, and refine the system prompt when the results need adjustment.',
        icon: 'tabler:adjustments',
      },
      {
        title: 'Your Assignment',
        description:
          'A practical capstone: learners use their Gem to generate a real weekly status report with their own work context. The assignment bridges course learning and immediate on-the-job application.',
        icon: 'tabler:clipboard-check',
      },
      {
        title: 'Reflection and Next Steps',
        description:
          'Closes the course with a reflection activity and forward-looking guidance — helping learners identify other recurring tasks where a Gem could save time and build a habit of continued AI skill development.',
        icon: 'tabler:arrow-right',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Course Embed -->
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="text-center mb-10">
      <p class="text-base text-secondary dark:text-blue-200 font-bold tracking-wide uppercase mb-2">
        Interactive Course
      </p>
      <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tighter font-heading">Try the Course</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-muted mt-4">
        The full Rise 360 course — self-paced, interactive, with knowledge checks throughout. Best experienced on
        desktop.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="relative w-full rounded-lg shadow-lg overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"
      style="padding-top: 62.5%;"
    >
      <iframe
        class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full"
        src="/gemini-course"
        title="Work Smarter with Gemini — Rise 360 Course"
        frameborder="0"
        allowfullscreen></iframe>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted text-center mt-4">
      If the course doesn't load in the embed, <a
        href="/gemini-course"
        target="_blank"
        class="text-accent hover:underline">open it directly</a
      >.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Design Decisions -->
  <Content tagline="Design Approach" title="What shaped the instructional design decisions">
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-6">A few intentional choices define how this course is structured:</p>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div class="flex gap-3">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent text-white flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold mt-0.5"
          >
            1
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm">Scope limited to Gem creation, not all of Gemini.</p><p
              class="text-sm text-muted mt-1"
            >
              A course that tries to teach everything about Gemini ends up teaching nothing well. This course starts
              with just enough context to make sense of Gems, then goes deep on the one thing that changes behavior:
              building and using a Gem for a task you do every week.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent text-white flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold mt-0.5"
          >
            2
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm">Healthcare context throughout — not generic business scenarios.</p><p
              class="text-sm text-muted mt-1"
            >
              Employees at a large health organization have a specific work context: compliance awareness, high
              documentation load, and recurring administrative tasks. The examples and use cases reflect that reality,
              which makes the content feel relevant rather than theoretical.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent text-white flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold mt-0.5"
          >
            3
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm">
              Consistent lesson structure reduces cognitive load for self-paced learners.
            </p><p class="text-sm text-muted mt-1">
              Every lesson follows the same pattern — concept introduction, interactive element, applied context,
              knowledge check. When learners know what to expect from each lesson, they spend less mental energy
              navigating the course and more actually processing the content.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-3">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-accent text-white flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-bold mt-0.5"
          >
            4
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm">Ending with a real deliverable, not a final quiz.</p><p
              class="text-sm text-muted mt-1"
            >
              The capstone assignment asks learners to use their Gem with their own actual work — a real status report,
              not a sample scenario. That transfer from course to job is the outcome that matters, and designing
              explicitly for it is what makes training stick.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-d"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Learning Measurement Plan -->
  <Content tagline="Measurement Plan" title="How I'd know if it worked" isReversed>
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full py-12 px-6">
        <div class="w-full max-w-xs space-y-3">
          {
            [
              {
                level: 'L1',
                label: 'Reactions',
                detail: 'Did learners find it relevant and usable?',
                color: 'blue',
              },
              {
                level: 'L2',
                label: 'Learning',
                detail: 'Can they build and use a Gem correctly?',
                color: 'green',
              },
              {
                level: 'L3',
                label: 'Behavior',
                detail: 'Are they using Gems 30–60 days later?',
                color: 'purple',
              },
              {
                level: 'L4',
                label: 'Results',
                detail: 'Is recurring task time decreasing?',
                color: 'orange',
              },
            ].map(({ level, label, detail, color }) => (
              <div
                class={`flex items-start gap-3 p-3 rounded-lg border ${color === 'blue' ? 'border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-800 bg-blue-50 dark:bg-blue-900/20' : color === 'green' ? 'border-green-200 dark:border-green-800 bg-green-50 dark:bg-green-900/20' : color === 'purple' ? 'border-purple-200 dark:border-purple-800 bg-purple-50 dark:bg-purple-900/20' : 'border-orange-200 dark:border-orange-800 bg-orange-50 dark:bg-orange-900/20'}`}
              >
                <span
                  class={`text-xs font-bold px-2 py-1 rounded flex-shrink-0 ${color === 'blue' ? 'bg-blue-600 text-white' : color === 'green' ? 'bg-green-600 text-white' : color === 'purple' ? 'bg-purple-600 text-white' : 'bg-orange-600 text-white'}`}
                >
                  {level}
                </span>
                <div>
                  <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">{label}</p>
                  <p class="text-xs text-muted mt-0.5">{detail}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-6">
        A course that can't be measured can't be improved. If this course were deployed inside a health organization,
        here's how I'd close the loop — using the Kirkpatrick model as the evaluation framework:
      </p>
      <div class="space-y-5">
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mb-1">
            Level 1 — Reactions <span class="font-normal text-muted">(immediate)</span>
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            A 5-item post-course survey in Typeform or Google Forms measuring satisfaction, perceived relevance, and
            confidence: <em>"I could build a Gem for a task I do this week."</em> Triggered automatically at course completion
            in Rise 360. Baseline target: 80% of learners rating confidence at 4/5 or higher.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mb-1">
            Level 2 — Learning <span class="font-normal text-muted">(built into the course)</span>
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Knowledge checks are already embedded throughout. The capstone assignment — building and using a real Gem
            with their own work — is the primary L2 evidence: learners either produce a working Gem or they don't. Any
            knowledge check with a high failure rate signals a lesson that needs redesign.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mb-1">
            Level 3 — Behavior Transfer <span class="font-normal text-muted">(30 and 60 days post-training)</span>
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            A brief manager check-in or self-report survey: <em
              >"Which recurring tasks are you using a Gem for? How often?"</em
            > This is the hardest data to collect and the most meaningful. A course that scores well on L1 and L2 but shows
            no L3 behavior change points to a transfer problem — usually a gap between course scenarios and real work context.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-semibold text-sm text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mb-1">
            Level 4 — Results <span class="font-normal text-muted">(organizational)</span>
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Harder to attribute directly, but trackable indicators include: number of Gems created per employee at 60
            days, self-reported time saved on administrative tasks, and overall AI tool adoption rate in the department.
            These would require partnership with an IT or operations team to track.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Reflection -->
  <Content tagline="Reflection" title="Turning a training experience into a design artifact" isReversed>
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full py-12">
        <div class="text-center">
          <p class="text-8xl font-bold font-heading text-accent leading-none">Rise</p>
          <p class="text-xl text-muted mt-3">Articulate Rise 360</p>
          <div class="mt-8 space-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
            <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Portfolio · LearningMate</p>
            <p>AI Adoption · Google Gemini</p>
            <p>Healthcare · Gemini Gems</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">
        Going through the Gem creation training at LearningMate as a learner was a useful vantage point. I noticed what
        made the training effective, where it left gaps, and how it could be adapted for a specific organizational
        context — a large health organization where employees have a clear recurring-task problem that Gems are
        well-suited to solve.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-4">
        That's where this portfolio piece came from: taking a training experience, identifying what a more contextually
        grounded version of it would look like for a specific audience, and building it. The result is a course that's
        narrower in scope than the original training — by design — because a narrower scope with a concrete outcome is
        more likely to change behavior than a broad survey of features.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {
          [
            'Rise 360',
            'eLearning',
            'AI Adoption',
            'Google Gemini',
            'Gemini Gems',
            'Healthcare Learning',
            'Instructional Design',
            'LearningMate',
            'Self-Paced Learning',
            'Workplace Training',
          ].map((tag) => (
            <span class="bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-900 text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-200 text-sm font-medium px-3 py-1 rounded-full">
              {tag}
            </span>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-4">
        Portfolio project — Rise 360 course on Gemini Gem creation for a large health organization. Developed following
        Gemini training at LearningMate.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <CallToAction
    actions={[
      { variant: 'primary', text: 'View All Projects', href: '/projects' },
      { text: 'Get in Touch', href: '/about#contact' },
    ]}
  >
    <Fragment slot="title">Interested in working together?</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle"
      >I'm open to remote opportunities in customer education, technical instructional design, and product enablement.</Fragment
    >
  </CallToAction>
</Layout>
